(Phnom Penh): Dr. Chhim Sarath, Asia Director of the AIDS Health Care Foundation (AHF), says that girls and young women are important for many reasons in managing hygiene during menstruation, especially Sanitary pads need them to be able to move freely wherever they want, such as participating in social activities, studying and participating in the community.
The AHF is helping those who can help and is also raising the voice and support of the estimated 500 million girls and women who do not have access to proper menstrual hygiene facilities, he said. The AHF calls on lawmakers at all levels to prioritize the issue of children, women and women in order to better understand and provide the necessary services and sanitation infrastructure that girls and women need.
Dr. Ly Penh Sun, President of the National Center for AIDS, Dermatology and Sexually Transmitted Infections, said that facilitating and encouraging and educating girls about menstrual hygiene and especially in the first place of life. It is very important for them to be healthy so that they can become healthy mothers in caring for their children and families and the nation.
Dr. Ly Penh Sun also praised the initiative of the AIDS Health Care Foundation, which made us all think about all these issues that have been overlooked in the past.
