Cambodia builds first safe shelter building in Tonle Sap province to facilitate flood evacuation
(Kampong Thom): Cambodia is a country that is flooded by the Mekong River and floods almost every year. The floods not only affected crops but also flooded people's homes.
Hundreds of families were evacuated during the floods. Their challenges when evacuating to safety are lack of shelter, lack of toilets and lack of clean water.
To address this challenge, the Royal Government of Cambodia, through the National Committee for Disaster Management, with the support of the World Food Organization under the auspices of the Government of Japan, has decided to build a shelter to facilitate the evacuation of people during the evacuation. Flood.
Under the auspices of the National Committee for Disaster Management and the World Food Program, under the auspices of the Government of Japan, six shelters have been completed at a cost of more than 1 million US dollars.
The provinces where the shelter was built include Pursat, Kampong Thom and Kampong Chhnang. This safe place is made in accordance with international standards, equipped with lightning protection equipment, kitchen, bathroom for men and women, water supply, laundry and laundry room, hand wash, playground, warehouse. And equip generators.
The Safe Building in Pursat Province was inaugurated on May 21, 2021. The safe building in Kampong Thom province was inaugurated on May 22, 2021 under the chairmanship of Senior Minister Kun Kim, First Vice President of the National Committee for Disaster Management, Mr. Karimata Atsushi, Assistant Secretary of the Embassy of Japan, and Ms. Claire Konan, Director. World Food Program in Cambodia.
The event was also attended by Mr. Sok Lou, Governor of Kampong Thom Province, as well as government officials in some other provinces.
Senior Minister Kun Kim congratulated the achievements of these safe buildings. He said that the Royal Government of Cambodia, led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, has always considered the fight against disasters. In addition, every year when there is a disaster such as floods in 2021, Samdech Techo Hun Sen and officials as officials to agree to help people.
Senior Minister Kun Kim called the establishment of the shelter a step in the right direction and response of the Royal Government of Cambodia to address the difficulties of the people in the face of floods.
"Natural disasters in the world are so acute that it is difficult to ban them," he said. But all we can do is find a way to respond to the disaster in a timely manner. In particular, help people from the disaster so that there are no deaths and have adequate accommodation.
"This safe haven is the government's response to the disaster to provide adequate temporary shelter when they evacuate their homes." This was further confirmed by Senior Minister Kun Kim.
"Cambodia is one of the countries most vulnerable to climate change, such as floods and droughts," said Claire Conan, WFP Country Director for Cambodia. She said the establishment of these centers was part of a long-term partnership between the World Food Program and the National Committee for Disaster Management and to strengthen the country's preparedness and emergency response.
Japan is a strong partner of the World Food Program. Over the last decade, Japan has contributed more than $ 22 million to improving food security and nutrition and disaster relief in Cambodia.
The Japanese-funded project, implemented by the World Food Program (WFP), aims to reduce disaster risk in flood-prone areas, such as the construction of evacuation centers, said Karimata Atsushi, a Japanese embassy assistant. , Training officers on disaster risk management, including risk assessment and emergency response, and establishing a disaster management information system for evacuation.
Karimata Atsushi added that the Japanese government is proud to help the Cambodian community build resilience to natural disasters. The senior Japanese embassy official also suggested that Cambodia be better prepared to deal with disasters, such as conducting exercises to prepare for disaster relief.
The Japanese ambassador said that Cambodia is now facing an even more difficult situation not only due to the floods, but also the Kovid 19 pandemic. He hopes that these centers will not only be used for evacuation during floods, but also for other necessities, such as vaccinations.
Senior Minister Kun Kim thanked the World Food Program and the Government of Japan for their continued support for disaster management and response, such as funding for the construction of the shelter.
He called on the World Food Program (WFP) and the Japanese government to continue to build safe shelters in the provinces bordering the Tonle Sap Lake and along the Mekong River, which are all flood-prone areas.
On that occasion, Senior Minister Kun Kim reminded the officials of the National Committee for Disaster Management and provincial officials to take good care of this safe building to serve the needs in the event of a disaster.
