Pursat Province Launches Two Shelter Buildings for Flood Victims
(Pursat): Pursat province has put into operation two safe shelters for people to live in during floods. Both shelters were established in collaboration with the World Food Program and the National Committee for Disaster Management.
Pursat, a province bordering the Tonle Sap Lake and major rivers with a network of waterfalls from the mountains, is prone to flooding almost every year. In 2020, Pursat province was flooded by heavy rains from the mountains.
At the inauguration ceremony of the safe building on May 21, 2021, Mr. Mao Thanin, Governor of Pursat Province, said that people in some communes in Bakan and Kandeung districts are often affected by floods and Extremely difficult, lack of safety when evacuating from the village during floods.
The provincial governor pointed out that the two towers were built at a cost of about 440,000 US dollars, supported by the Japanese government. This safe building is located in Russey Taman village, Boeung Bot Kandol commune, Bakan district, with an area of 864 square meters, built on state public land.
Another location is behind Kdei Udom pagoda, Kdei Lath village, Sre Stok commune, Kandeung district, with a building size of 1685.06 square meters, built on land donated by the chief abbot of Kdei Udom pagoda. The construction of this safe building started in 2020 and was completed in April 2021, recently under the direct supervision of the National Committee for Disaster Management, provincial authorities, districts, communes and the World Food Organization.
Due to the lack of infrastructure, this safe place has made it very difficult for people to live, both defecate and use clean water. The shelter is equipped with all these infrastructures that facilitate the accommodation of the time during the evacuation of its residence.
The shelter is built according to international standards and is equipped with essential facilities such as kitchen, laundry, storage room, bathroom, hand wash, animal shelter, plumbing and lightning protection system.
Mr. Mao Thanin said that this safe building is very important for the people in the flood-affected villages and communes who can evacuate the flood-affected houses to stay temporarily until the flood subsides.
According to Emma Conlan, the World Food Program (WFP) Country Representative in Cambodia, Cambodia is one of the countries most vulnerable to climate change, such as floods and droughts.
She said the establishment of these centers is part of a long-term partnership between the World Food Program and the National Committee for Disaster Management to reduce disaster risk and strengthen emergency preparedness and response in the country.
With this safe haven, Mr. Lam Heng Huot, Deputy Chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management (NCDD), on behalf of General Kun Kim, Senior Minister, First Deputy Chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management, praised The efforts of the Capital and Provincial Disaster Management Committee on disaster response in the past have continued to adhere to the principle of community-level and family-level preparedness plans.
As the Chairman of the Provincial Disaster Management Committee, Mr. Mao Thanin instructed the relevant district and commune authorities, especially the management committee of the two safe buildings, to pay attention to the management and maintenance of the safe building, which is a new achievement for the people. Citizens in their communities well. In particular, perform its duties and responsibilities consistently as defined in each sub-committee to manage the safety building.
