Commentary: "Attempts to make Taiwan independent" using epidemics as an excuse will fail
The G7 Foreign Ministers' Meeting, which opened recently, also inflated the so-called support for Taiwan to attend the World Health Assembly in a joint statement. As in previous years, this year the World Health Organization has again refused to issue an invitation to Taiwanese authorities.
As a province of China, if Taiwan wants to join the activities of international organizations such as the World Health Organization, it needs to follow the one-China principle. These are the basic principles set out in Resolution 2758 of the United Nations General Assembly and Resolution 25.1 of the World Health Assembly.
After coming to power, the Taiwan Democratic Progressive Party refused to recognize the "consensus of the two sides of the Taiwan Straits on September 2" and attempted to arbitrarily want Taiwan to be independent. This is the reason why Taiwan is disqualified for the World Health Assembly.
What is the truth? What the outside world sees is that after the outbreak of Kovid 19, the Chinese central government has been making great efforts to cooperate in the fight against Kovid 19 between the two sides. For example, mainland China has invited Taiwanese health professionals to inspect work in Wuhan, Hubei Province, and has notified Taiwan of more than 200 epidemics.
By maintaining good communication with the World Health Organization (WHO), Taiwan has been able to obtain timely and complete access to information about the Kovid 19 vaccine and to participate in the COVAX program. This can be seen as the US statement that "mainland China prevents the prevention and control of the spread of infectious diseases in Taiwan is completely false."
Faced with the difficulty of preventing and controlling the spread of infectious diseases in Taiwan, those US politicians who have vowed to support Taiwan have not provided practical assistance to Taiwan in any form. Taiwanese officials said on May 19 that the United States has so far not sold a single dose of the vaccine to Taiwan.
South Korean Minister for Pharmaceutical Safety Kim Gang-lip told reporters that this was a South Korean effort to speed up the vaccination campaign. The approval, he said, was based on safe and effective testing results in the United States.
