(Pursat): The flames of war in Cambodia for nearly four decades from the late 1960s to the late 1990s have devastated Cambodian families, deprived them of their lives and left many disabled. The flames of war have also left many Cambodian veterans living with disabilities to this day.
Despite being disabled until now, Sarath is not disappointed. He considers this a sacrifice for his homeland.
The win-win policy set by Samdech Techo Hun Sen put out the flames of war in Cambodia and found the light of complete peace in 1998. This peace has given Cambodian society the opportunity to build and develop and have better and better economic growth.
More than 20 years after the end of the Cambodian war and the growing economy, Cambodian veterans who have sacrificed for the cause of the nation continue to live with disabilities, and most veterans have no chance. Build a better life from this goal of peace.
However, not all veterans are left alone. Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, and Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian Red Cross, paid great attention to the lives and livelihoods of veterans and their families.
In return for the great contribution to the national cause of the veterans in Pursat province, Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Kittiprittbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen have assigned Senior Minister Kun Kim, the Veterans Affairs Officer, to investigate. Find the needy veterans in Pursat province and arrange to provide them with land and build houses.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen has decided to provide more than 900 hectares of social land concessions in Rany Sen Chey village, Damnak Troyeung, Prongel commune, Phnom Kravanh district, Pursat province to provide to 628 families of veterans. One family of veterans received 1.5 hectares of land.
Currently, about 60 veterans' houses have been built and are gradually being allowed to move in. Village planning and construction of houses for veterans have received high attention from General Kun Kim, Senior Minister, who is the Vice President and Secretary General of the Association.
Mr. Mao Thanin, Governor of Pursat Province and Honorary President of the Cambodian Veterans Association of Pursat Province also paid close attention to this work. As a provincial leader who has a high responsibility for the well-being of the people in the province, Mr. Mao Thanin regularly inspects and promotes the process of organizing the village and the construction of houses.
On the area where 60 houses have been built, Mr. Mao Thanin took the army in the province to help prepare the fence of the house, clear the land, plant fruits such as mangoes, coconuts and oranges for the veterans. In addition, there is land clearing and infrastructure such as roads, toilets and digging wells.
In addition to providing land and building houses, the authorities are also considering helping to improve the lives of veterans who will be living here. According to Mr. Mao Thanin, the land provided for the veterans is all fertile land and highly suitable for cultivation.
So he prepared seeds for the veterans to plant in the rainy season this year. In addition, Mr. Mao Thanin also decided to support a family of 50 kg of rice for a year to support the lives of veterans who came to settle first. This is to ensure that the families of veterans do not face a lack of livelihood.
Although the new location, which is a warm shelter for veterans in Rany Sen Chey village, Damnak Troyeung, Prongel commune, Phnom Kravanh district, is not yet ready, but some veterans' families have gradually moved in. With the resettlement, they began to set up houses and grow crops on land with high agricultural potential.
Yom Sophay, 67, is a veteran who fought on the battlefield with Khmer Rouge soldiers in the 1980s. Mr. Yom Sophay broke down in tears when asked about receiving the land and the house. Mr. Sophay's tears express his excitement for the opportunity to get land and a house that he never imagined.
Mr. Yom Sophay considered the gift of land and house from Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittipritthbandit as the most meaningful and important gift for his life. He claimed that he had instructed his children not to sell the land and the house, even though they were poor.
Another family of veterans, Mr. Song Sarath's family, who brought their children to live on the land and house donated by Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittipritthbandit, are enjoying the new life support. The veteran expressed his excitement and also revealed plans to build a better new life on this land and house.
Mao Sivutha, 59, is also another veteran who received land and houses from Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittiprittbandit. Sivutha is a former soldier in Division 4 stationed on the border of Koh Kong and Trat provinces in Thailand. In 1987, Mr. Sivutha stepped on a landmine and was paralyzed in one leg until today.
Mr. Mao Sivutha expressed his gratitude and appreciation to Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Kittipritthbandit, an unforgettable day.
Mr. Mao Thanin, as the Governor and Manager of the People in Pursat Province, on behalf of the veterans in the province, expressed his gratitude and will always remember the merits of Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittipritthbandit in providing land and houses. A warm haven for veterans.
There are 628 families of veterans in Pursat who will be living on this new land. But at present, the budget for building houses is around 200 houses. So there is still a lack of funds to build more houses.
