The conflict between Israel and Hamas has entered its second week, with both sides showing no intention of easing, despite calls for an international ceasefire. As of Tuesday, 212 Palestinians, including 61 children, had been killed in Israeli airstrikes on Gaza, while 10 Israelis, including one child, had been killed in an airstrike. Rockets fired by Hamas in Gaza.
According to the United Nations, the conflict between Israel and Hamas has caused a humanitarian crisis, forcing 40,000 Palestinians to become refugees and 2,500 to be left homeless. The Israeli military also claimed that Hamas fired 3,350 rockets into Israeli territory, but 90 percent of them were destroyed by Israeli air defense systems.
The UN Security Council is set to convene an emergency meeting on Tuesday to discuss the current situation between Israel and the Palestinians. But there is little hope that this top international body can issue a joint statement, as the United States, Israel's main backbone, has already vetoed the statement three times in the past. Only one week ago.
Australian trade minister, ambassador meet to discuss trade
(Phnom Penh): The Minister of Commerce, Mr. Pan Sosak, and Mr. Pablo Kang, Australian Ambassador to Cambodia, met on May 18, 2021 through a video conference on strengthening international trade cooperation. Between Cambodia and Australia. In particular, Australia's continued support for the development of Cambodian trade in bilateral, regional and multilateral frameworks.
During the meeting, the Minister of Commerce and the Ambassador discussed and exchanged views on developments in the context of multilateral trade under the framework of the World Trade Organization (WTO). In particular, preparations for the upcoming 12th WTO Ministerial Conference.
In the bilateral framework, the two sides exchanged views and discussed on the continued implementation of trade development projects under the auspices of Australia, including the strengthening and capacity building of trade officials, the development and development of trade-related legal standards. Continue to implement the Cambodia-Australia Agricultural Value Chain (CAVAC) program and promote e-commerce.
The Minister of Commerce thanked the Australian side for its continued support and significant contribution to the development of Cambodia's trade and related sectors. The two sides reaffirmed their commitment to increase bilateral trade, enhance cooperation in trade and all related fields, especially close ties between the two countries.
Australia is one of Cambodia's good trading partners. By 2020, despite the crisis caused by 19 Kovid, trade between the two countries will remain high.
By 2020, Cambodia and Australia will have a trade volume of nearly 214 million US dollars. Of that, Cambodia exported nearly 134 million dollars to Australia and imported from Australia nearly 80 million dollars.
