(Pursat): The Cambodian Veterans Association and the Pursat Provincial Authority have been speeding up their work to organize villages and build houses for veterans in Pursat so that they can move in soon.
New Villages and Homes for Cambodian Veterans, organized by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, and Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian Red Cross, located in Rany Sen Chey Village, Damnak Troyeung, Prongel Commune, Phnom Kravanh District, Pursat Province. The village is about 40 km from National Road 5 and about 80 km from the provincial town.
Veterans are the ones who have sacrificed the most in the cause of society during the crisis of war. Some of them became paralyzed, limbs and eyes. Although Cambodian society has found the light of peace for many years, the national economic growth is high, but some Cambodian veterans still can not build a better life. They are still living a life of scarcity.
Through the Veterans Association, with General Kun Kim, Senior Minister as Deputy Chairman, Secretary-General and Secretary-General, Samdech Techo Hun Sen has always reminded and instructed to search for needy veterans and prepare to help them.
Through this research, the Cambodian Veterans Association has found that more than 600 families of veterans in Pursat province are living in poverty, some with landless houses and others without houses.
In order to facilitate the living and improve the livelihood of these veterans, Samdech Techo Hun Sen decided to take more than 900 hectares of state land in Rany Sen Chey village, Damnak Troyeung, Prongel commune, Phnom Kravanh district, Pursat province to provide a policy to more than 600 veterans. This family. One family received 1.5 hectares of land.
In addition, Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen also donated $ 500,000 to help build a house, and some philanthropists also contributed through the appeal of the Pursat Veterans Association.
The money is sponsored by Samdech Kittipritthbandit and philanthropists, making it possible for the association to build around 200 houses. Currently, the team has built about 60 houses, and some of the houses are also occupied by veterans.
The house is 5 meters by 7 meters and 3 meters down to the kitchen. The house is built of galvanized iron roof, smart walls, metal components, and cement columns.
With the construction of houses, the Cambodian Veterans Association and provincial authorities have been busy preparing housing for veterans in the area, such as clearing land, building roads, cultivating crops and digging water wells.
The team and the authorities also prepared mango trees, coconut trees, etc. to plant for the veterans on the land that belongs to them.
The construction of the house for the veterans was led by Senior Minister Kun Kim and Mr. Mao Thanin, Governor of Pursat Province.
The senior minister as well as the governor of Pursat province regularly visit to speed up the construction and preparation of the village so that the veterans can move in. On the morning of May 17, 2021, Senior Minister Kun Kim and Mr. Mao Thanin inspected and promoted the construction.
Troops in Pursat province have been deployed at the site to clear land, build houses and help plant fruit trees for veterans.
Senior Minister Kun Kim emphasized that Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittipritthbandit regularly monitored the work of organizing the village and building the house. The Senior Minister emphasized that Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittipritthbandit paid great attention to the well-being of Cambodian veterans, who are all meritorious for the motherland.
Mr. Mao Thanin, representing the veterans in his province, expressed his happiness and gratitude to Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittipritthbandit for their attention and compassion for the needy veterans.
For Mr. Mao Thanin, the merit is huge and both he and the veterans in the province will never forget and continue to support Samdech Techo and Samdech Kittipritthbandit as the leader of Cambodia forever.
According to the plan, about 60 houses will be handed over to the veterans at the end of May.
Mr. Mao Thanin confirmed that with this entry, Mr. Keo Rattanak, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and the Director General of Electricite du Cambodge also provided solar panels in the back of the house for cooking, transporting and using lamps.
With this first move, Mr. Mao Thanin also helps supply 50 kg of rice per family per month for one year.
