Please be informed that every year on May 20, the Cambodian People's Party always commemorates the souls of the Cambodian people who died due to the genocidal regime of Pol Pot, especially as a celebration to dedicate merit to those who have The dead lost those lives as well.
May 20 Remembrance Day was called the "Day of Anger" by the National Assembly of the People's Republic of Kampuchea in 1983 in remembrance of the Khmer Rouge regime's massacre of the Cambodian people. May 20, 1973 was the birthday of the cooperatives of the Khmer Rouge Democratic Kampuchea regime, and the war continued until it reached Phnom Penh on April 17, 1975.
"National Day of Remembrance" has just been designated by the Royal Government of Cambodia as a national holiday on February 14, 2018. This day is designated as an official national holiday to commemorate the victims and victims of the crimes committed during the Democratic Kampuchea regime from April 17, 1975 to January 6, 1979. 284 Families in Boribo District Receive 10 Tons of Rice Seed from King Kong Sam Ol for Cultivation
