The following is the King's message: An overview of Cambodia-Russia relations for 65 years
For the past 65 years, relations between Cambodia and the Soviet Union, later transformed into the Russian Federation, have been influenced by both domestic and regional and global politics. Although the two countries are geographically far apart, the people of the two countries have lived together intimately as cooperative partners based on the principles of mutual support and respect.
Historically, relations between the two countries can be divided into five major stages and evolve.
From 1956 to 1970, relations between Cambodia and the Soviet Union were unique. In the early years of this relationship, Soviet leaders greatly appreciated Norodom Sihanouk's diligent efforts to transform Cambodia into an independent state in which the people lived in peace and prosperity. The Soviet Union then supported the initiative of Norodom Sihanouk, the co-founder of the Non-Aligned Movement.
In 1957, Cambodia and the Soviet Union signed a trade agreement and cultural and scientific cooperation. The Soviet Union also supported Cambodia's membership in the United Nations. On the other hand, the Soviet Union reaffirmed its allegiance to the Cambodian people by condemning the 1970 Lon Nol military coup.
From 1979 to 1991, the two countries had good relations. In early 1979, diplomatic relations between the two countries were re-established. From 1980 to 1990, the Soviet Union provided extensive economic and military assistance, as well as technical assistance to Cambodia.
From 1991 to 1993, relations between the two countries were at a crossroads. After the fall of the Berlin Wall and the break-up of the former Soviet Union in 1991, Russia's overall influence in Southeast Asia diminished rapidly. In December 1991, Sihanouk announced the recognition of the Russian Federation as the successor state of the Soviet Union.
Since 1993, relations between the two countries have been significantly strengthened and expanded in politics, diplomacy, economy and people-to-people contacts.
In politics, Cambodia and Russia are trustworthy partners with good political cooperation in solving common problems in the world and in the region. The two countries are cooperating in international and regional structures and frameworks, including interactions within the United Nations and relevant institutions and ASEAN forums.
In the field of diplomacy, high-level visits are frequently observed during 2015 and 2016. In 2015, there were many official visits by Cambodian leaders to Russia, including the visit of the former Minister of Foreign Affairs of Cambodia, Deputy Prime Minister Hor Namhong in March, Samdech Vibol Panha Sok An, Samdech Kheng In May and Mr. Say Sam Al, Minister of Environment in October.
More importantly, the first visit of the Prime Minister of the Russian Federation to Cambodia in November 2015, and the visit of Samdech Techo Prime Minister Hun Sen to Russia in May 2016. For the first time in 30 years, this marks the beginning of a new high-level relationship.
In the economic field, bilateral trade between the two countries has increased significantly in the last decade. According to data from the Russian Federal Customs, imports from Russia from Cambodia last year amounted to more than 200 million US dollars, and exports from Russia to Cambodia were close to 20 million US dollars.
In terms of people-to-people contacts, more than 60,000 Russian tourists visit Cambodia every year. Meanwhile, there are also some Russians living in Cambodia, most of whom live in Phnom Penh, Siem Reap and Sihanoukville. More importantly, more than 8,000 Cambodians have studied in the former Soviet Union and Russia since 1979.
Issues related to the Russian vaccine "Sputnik V" were also raised during a meeting between the Russian Ambassador to Cambodia and Prime Minister Hun Sen. The embassy immediately notified the relevant Russian authorities of the talks with Samdech Techo Prime Minister Hun Sen. The working groups of both countries have been working on this issue.
In conclusion, Cambodia-Russia relations are bound by long-standing traditional friendship and mutual respect. Both countries have similar views on world order. Cambodia and Russia have many years of experience in cooperating with each other.
The two countries have made fruitful interactions on the world stage. It is clear that over the past 65 years, the traditional friendship between Cambodia and Russia has grown steadily in various fields. However, the volume of bilateral trade remains limited and it is hoped that Cambodia's request for the Covid-19 vaccine will receive a positive response.
It is believed that relations between the two countries will continue to grow stronger and diversify into more areas. The solidarity between the two countries in resolving international challenges emerges, strong experience in bilateral dialogue, the acceleration of all relations, all forms of deep-rooted understanding and mutual respect will surely Bring greater achievements both nationally and globally.
