(Phnom Penh): As a rule of law in a civil case, the participation of the parties or both parties between the plaintiff and the defendant is necessary and very important at all stages of court proceedings. In court proceedings, the parties may assert and present their claims, as well as respond to the claims of the other party.
This claim can be made orally or by other documents, but in order for the court to have greater confidence in its claim, the parties should make a claim or respond to their claim by submitting a document in The Code of Civil Procedure is called the "Prepared Document." When can the parties file this preparation and what are the purposes and benefits of the court proceedings in the case?
To better understand Fresh Legal Awareness Program in collaboration with the Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation this week, the topic of "Prepared Documents" is presented to the following students:
During the proceedings of a civil case, the parties often make their own arguments and appeals, regardless of time. Because the claims of the parties may be complicated or can be repeated, the Code of Civil Procedure provides for the filing of documents that specify specific points to protect the claims and assertions of the two parties called "Prepared documents."
Provisions on prepared documents are provided in Articles 101 and 102 of the Code of Civil Procedure, which collectively state the power of the court to order the parties to submit the prepared documents as well as the contents of the plaintiffs' prepared documents; The end of the answer, too. A preparatory document is a document that contains specific points to prevent claims or confrontations between parties.
Prepared documents are important for expeditious litigation, as the court relies on this document to prepare the parties' claims, clarify the point of dispute of the case, prepare evidence related to the point of dispute, as well as the facts with or without dispute. To reach the next stage of the procedure. The number of prepared documents is not limited, as in some complex cases, multiple prepared documents are also filed.
Defendant's filing is done to:
1: Respond to the content of the verdict that the plaintiff wrote in the complaint. 2: Recognition or non-recognition of the facts written in the complaint. Write in the plaintiff's complaint. 4: Respond to the plaintiff's prepared documents.
The filing of the plaintiff's prepared documents is to:
1: Recognize or reject the facts of the protest in case the respondent confirms the facts of the protest. 2: Protest against the statement of the respondent. Answer 4: Confirm other facts that are useful to you.
