In a letter from the Council of Ministers, it was stated that the three major risk factors for the implementation of the national vaccination campaign strategic plan are:
* First: Inadequate access to vaccines: Limited supply of vaccines and high demand from countries around the world may pose a risk in obtaining vaccines to meet Cambodia's specific needs. In addition, the complexity of technical issues in the management and supply of vaccines may also delay the implementation of this strategic plan by 2021 as soon as possible.
This requires the Royal Government to work hard to ensure adequate sources for the purchase and supply of vaccines on demand and on a regular basis, as well as to build capacity to prepare for technical issues related to management, distribution, transportation and maintenance. Vaccines, etc. that may occur eventually.
However, the right decision of the Royal Government to take strategic partners with Chinese friends as the core for seeking to buy vaccines and get vaccine assistance has also become an important factor that can help address the risk of scarcity. Adequate and timely vaccination and supply of vaccines.
Second, the ability to implement a vaccination plan: the large number of people who need to be vaccinated and the short duration of the national vaccination campaign can lead to challenges and a lack of institutional and human resource capacity. Ability to participate in national vaccinations effectively and timely.
This requires good preparation of a detailed plan for implementation in all aspects and mobilization, as well as the use of total national strength and means to participate in the implementation of this national vaccination campaign most effectively.
* Third: The emergence or infection in Cambodia of the 19 new Kovid virus: A new strain of the virus is a concern that may make the existing vaccine disappear or become ineffective.
