What are the important points of this country that need to be understood? For more information, please read the following general knowledge article:
India has an area of 3,287,590 square kilometers, the seventh largest in the world, according to Statista. It is located in Southwest Asia, bordering the Arabian Sea to the east, bordering the Bay of Bengal and the Indian Ocean to the south, and bordering on the north, northeast and northwest. And Pakistan, Nepal, Bhutan, China and Bangladesh.
Hindi, English and other languages are used in India. Hindi is spoken by the largest population (about 30% of the population) as the official language for the federation. English is widely used in business and administration as an official language for assistance. The Constitution also recognizes that the other 21 languages have extensive uses or representations of antiquity. India has 1,652 languages, the largest in the world.
The Constitution of India was promulgated on August 26, 1950. According to the Indian Constitution in the preamble, India is a republic with socialist sovereignty, independent of religion and democracy. India has a federal government and two parliaments, which share three different powers: the legislature, the executive and the judiciary.
The executive branch The executive branch consists of the president, vice president, and cabinet, headed by the prime minister. Every minister in the Council of Ministers must be a member of one of the two houses. In the Indian parliamentary system, the executive depends on the legislature. Therefore, the Prime Minister and his cabinet are accountable to the lower house.
* Indian courts have three sub-courts: the Supreme Court, headed by a Chief Justice, and 25 other judges appointed by the President to serve until the age of 65, or to be removed if evidence of wrongdoing is found. Unethical or unethical acts and 21 high courts (equivalent to the Court of Appeal in Cambodia) as well as many lower courts (equivalent to the Court of First Instance in Cambodia).
The Supreme Court has jurisdiction over cases involving fundamental rights and disputes between the state and the central authorities, as well as appeals from higher courts. The courts must be independent and have the power to enact laws and can also overturn federal or state laws that are unconstitutional. The role of final observer of the Constitution is an important function of the Supreme Court.
4. International Relations Since gaining independence in 1947, India has maintained relations with many nations. In the 1950s, India took a leading role in supporting the independence claims of European colonies in Asia and Africa.
After the Sino-Indian and Indo-Pakistani wars of 1965, close ties between India and the Soviet Union caused India to lose contact with the United States and continued until the end of the Cold War. India has fought four wars with Pakistan, the main issue being the dispute over Kashmir. India won the war with Pakistan in 1971, with India supporting the National Liberation Movement of Bangladesh (formerly East Pakistan).
India is a longtime member and supporter of the United Nations (UN) in 35 peacekeeping operations across the four continents. The idea, initiated by Indian leaders, has re-established US-Pakistan relations. In the economic field, India has established close ties with developing countries in South America, Asia and Africa.
5: Remarkable points According to the book of the Ministry of Education on India in the Middle Ages, published in 2020, said that the country has also made great achievements in science and many discoveries, especially in astronomy, mathematics, chemistry. And physics.
India became a nuclear power in 1974, after the first test without a public announcement, until 1998, when it tested nuclear weapons again and publicly declared its potential nuclear power.
According to Investopedia, by 2020, India will become the fifth richest economy in the world. The health sector has also made remarkable progress, but unfortunately during the 19th Kovid Crisis, India was unable to protect its people, even with insufficient oxygen, resulting in a series of deaths.
