No one knows the origin of the foot-and-mouth disease, only that it is noticed that the patient first develops small red rashes on the skin until the seventh day, the skin symptoms then develop into clear blisters. On the 21st day, the blisters become lumps on the patient's skin, which looks scary.
Historians who tracked the spread of foot-and-mouth disease claim that the disease was caused by the rise of European civilization and the discovery of new territories. On the other hand, the expansion of trade over the centuries also caused the foot-and-mouth disease to continue to spread.
The events of the outbreak of chickenpox can be summarized as follows:
In the 6th century, through the growth of trade between China and Korea, the spread of foot-and-mouth disease spread to Japan.
In the 7th century, through the spread of Arab influence, foot-and-mouth disease spread to North Africa, Spain, and Portugal.
In the 11th century, due to religious conflicts between Christians and Muslims, foot-and-mouth disease also spread to Europe.
In the 18th century, British explorers brought foot-and-mouth disease to Australia and Southeast Asia.
* Production of one of the first vaccines the world has ever known
In 1959, the World Health Organization (WHO) launched a project to eradicate foot-and-mouth disease. As well as the shortage of vaccine sponsorship. Despite their best efforts, foot-and-mouth disease continued to spread until 1966, when it spread throughout South America, Africa, and Asia.
The massive eradication of smallpox began in 1967 with renewed commitment. Laboratories in many countries where foot-and-mouth disease occurs have produced high-quality vaccines that can be stored at room temperature. There are many other factors that have contributed to the success of this program, including vaccination teams, the establishment of patient care systems and large-scale vaccination campaigns.
It should be recalled that during the first vaccination, Dr. Jenner hoped that his vaccine would be able to get rid of smallpox. Nearly two centuries later, the world's efforts were truly successful. At the 22nd session of the World Health Organization (WHO) on May 8, 1980, the World Health Organization (WHO) declared That foot-and-mouth disease no longer exists in the world.
