(Phnom Penh): Two factory workers in Kambol district admitted that they were confused about the vaccine information because of the polluted information on social media and prevented them from getting the vaccine in the first place.
After more than a year of harassment of Kovid 19, Cambodia, like other countries, has decided to launch a vaccination plan for its people as an important scenario in the fight against Kovid 19. Cambodia received the Sinofam vaccine, the first aid from the Chinese government, on February 7, 2021. After receiving the vaccine, Cambodia launched a vaccination campaign on February 10.
With the start of the Chinese-assisted vaccination campaign, there has been a movement to prevent people from going abroad for vaccinations by opposition groups led by Sam Rainsy. Opposition groups called for a cease-fire in protest of the government's plans to ban the use of Chinese vaccines.
Opposition misinformation spread on social media has affected people's decision to get vaccinated.
Another girl from Kampong Thom province also said that the misinformation about the vaccine accident made her refuse to be vaccinated. The worker confirmed that the information she received on Facebook was that the vaccine caused illness and health hazards.
On May 4, 2021, the two workers went to be vaccinated during the vaccination campaign in the red zone. The two workers confirmed that they decided to get vaccinated after seeing many people get vaccinated and there are no health problems.
After being vaccinated, the workers, who had been deceived by misinformation, expressed regret and confirmed that their health had not changed, that it was normal. After being vaccinated, the two workers also called on other workers to be vaccinated against Kovid 19 without having to worry about inciting some information.
With the first vaccination of the vaccine, the two women also thanked the Royal Government of Cambodia for seeking the vaccine to protect their health.
Vaccines are considered by the Royal Government of Cambodia as a strategic tool in the fight against Kovid 19 in addition to health protection measures. As of February 10, 2021, Cambodia has vaccinated nearly 1.8 million people with more than 4 million doses.
The Royal Government plans to vaccinate more than 10 million people by the first half of 2022 to build immunity against the spread of Kovid 19 in the country. Was fined after he clarified the reasons to the authorities
