Kampong Chhnang Provincial Administration Records the Case of Samaki Meanchey District Governor Shooting a Cow
The above report was sent to the Ministry of Interior after a few days ago, many social media sites reported the case of a dead cow in the area of Samaki Meanchey District Hall, Kampong Chhnang Province.
According to the spokesperson of Kampong Chhnang Provincial Administration, on April 30, 2021, at 3 pm, there was a case of a dead cow in Samaki Meanchey District Hall, which was released by its owner and came to eat on the campus. District. Police immediately inspected the scene and found the dead cow with a bullet in the head.
The spokesman said that according to the report of the Samaki Meanchey district administration and the forensic investigation of the police force, the cow died when Mr. Vin Sitha, the district governor, fired a shotgun and hit the ground, causing the bullet to hit. As for the firearm, it is the weapon responsible for maintaining the security of the district hall of the Samaki Meanchey District Administrative Unity Command.
Regarding this case, the Kampong Chhnang Provincial Administration has taken the following four steps to check and resolve:
1: Assign a team to search for the owner of the cow and coordinate to provide compensation of 4,000,000 Riels. At the same time, the team will educate and instruct the cow owners to be careful in raising and taking care of the animals properly, stop abandoning them in the open field, walking and destroying other people's crops as well as public property that leads to Conflict by any means and can affect traffic on the road, causing accidents as well.
2. The provincial administration assigned the police force to fill in the records in accordance with the procedures and confiscate the weapons and store them at the provincial police commission temporarily.
4: The provincial administration has prepared a report to the Ministry of Interior and to respect for guidance.
International Midwifery Day 2021: The role of midwives is crucial in saving lives, especially during the Kovid-19 outbreak
"On this auspicious occasion of the International Midwifery Day 2021, we reiterate that the role of midwives is crucial in saving lives, especially during the outbreak of Kovid disease," the statement said. 19 ".
Based on clear data and our role is simple but life-saving midwives, so investing in midwives contributes to reducing maternal and infant mortality.
International Midwifery Day is celebrated annually on May 5 with the aim of highlighting the important role of midwives and appreciation for their efforts to save the lives of mothers and babies. The theme for this year's International Midwifery Day is "Based on data on investing in midwifery."
This topic focuses on the important role of midwives as a basis for improving the quality of sexual, reproductive and maternal health care services and ensuring the universal access to these health services to achieve national health development goals and Commitment to the International Covenant on Population and Development and to achieve the Sustainable Development Goals by 2030.
Microfinance Association of Cambodia: More than 300,000 clients received over $ 3 billion in credit restructuring (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Mr. Kaing Tong Ngy, Director of Communications and Spokesperson of the Microfinance Association To date, more than 300,000 Kovid-affected clients have received more than $ 3 billion in credit restructuring, according to Cambodia.
Mr. Kaing Tong Ngy stated, "So far, the provision of additional credit in the financial sector as a whole (both banking and microfinance) is that we provide credit restructuring to more than 300,000 families in size. More than $ 3 billion in loans. ”
A spokesman for the Cambodian Microfinance Association added that the credit restructuring would continue as long as customers continued to be affected by Kovid-19.
In addition, Mr. Kaing Tong Ngy said that during March 2021, the request for credit restructuring of clients affected by the Kovid 19 crisis was higher than before the community event. February 20
He said that during the Kovid-19 epidemic, the Cambodian Microfinance Association urged all members not to suspend fines and sue clients or confiscate screenings, which is strictly prohibited. But feel that customers are suffering from Kovid-19 disease.
In addition, Mr. Kaing Tong Ngy emphasized that the Cambodian Microfinance Association has encouraged its members to continue to issue emergency loans or business restoration loans to people at a low interest rate.
According to the Cambodian Microfinance Association, microfinance institutions can only refinance their clients upon request from clients, in accordance with the contract between the institution and the client, the client protection policy, as well as certain legal conditions.
In the difficult circumstances caused by Kovid 19 disease, the Cambodian Microfinance Association has been actively working with its members and stakeholders, especially the National Bank of Cambodia, to help the affected clients.
