Myanmar's military says it can only respond to calls from ASEAN leaders if the country returns to "stability"
The military regime in Myanmar said on Tuesday it would respond to calls on ASEAN leaders to end violence as long as the country returns to stability amid fighting with rebels. Minorities also erupted along its eastern border. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on Tuesday, April 27, 2021.
Myanmar's military leader, General Min Aung Hlaing, attended a summit with 10 ASEAN leaders in Indonesia last weekend to try to resolve the crisis in his country. It was his first foreign trip since taking office.
As a result of the ASEAN Leaders' Summit, they issued a joint statement agreeing on five points, calling on the Myanmar military regime to immediately stop the violence against the protesters and allow a special envoy's visit. To this ASEAN country as well.
In response to the statement, the Myanmar Military Council responded that it would consider "constructive proposals made by the ASEAN leaders as the country's situation returns to stability."
The Myanmar military added that the suggestions of neighboring countries would be considered positive if (ASEAN) would facilitate the implementation of its five-step roadmap.
