Geographical history The Caspian Sea, like the Black Sea and the Mediterranean Sea, was once bordered by the oceans. It became closed about 5.5 million years ago due to continental drift, and salt concentrations decreased after being separated from the ocean. During hot and dry weather, the closed sea also dries up, but large amounts of salt grains are blown away from the dry sea surface, and during cold and wet weather the sea basin is replenished.
Due to the current freshwater flow, the Caspian Sea has become a freshwater lake in the north. It is highly saline off the coast of Iran, where reservoirs make up for freshwater flow. The salinity of the Caspian Sea is now about one-third of the Earth's oceans.
The divisions between the three regions are very different. The northern part includes only the upper cascade, with a very shallow depth of only 5-6 m, and the water volume is about 1% of the total lake volume. In the central part of the Caspian Sea, the average depth is about 190 meters. The southern region is the deepest region, more than 1,000 meters deep. The central and southern regions account for 33-66% of the total volume of the Caspian Sea.
